ebuilding it. Anything it doesn't remove is copied out exactly, so whitespace, quoting, attribute order, character codes, comments and the doctype all stay as they were. Ordinary content, including tables, headers and `<style>` blocks with normal CSS, is untouched.

**What it removes:**
- **Removed with their contents:** `<script>` and `<iframe>`, including mixed-case, inside SVG, and broken-up tricks like `<scr<script>ipt>`.
- **Removed, but what's inside them is kept:** `object`, `embed`, `applet`, `frame`, `frameset` and `base` tags.
- **Attributes:**
  - all `on*` event handlers (`onload`, `onerror`, …), including `/onerror=` written without a space;
  - `srcdoc`;
  - `style` attributes containing `expression(`, `javascript:`, `-moz-binding` or `behavior:`;
  - URL attributes using `javascript:`, `vbscript:` or similar, even when disguised with character codes, control characters or spaces;
  - `data:` URLs, unless they are images.
- **Other cases:**
  - `<meta>` refresh tags that redirect to `javascript:` or `data:`, and UTF-7 charset tricks;
  - `<link rel=import>`;
  - `<style>` blocks with dangerous CSS;
  - markup hidden inside comments, old Internet Explorer conditional comments, `<noscript>`, `<textarea>` and `<title>`.

**Side effects:**
- When it removes an attribute, it also removes the whitespace just before it (`<body onload=".." class="main">` becomes `<body class="main">`).
- A tag cut off by the end of the file is dropped.
- It runs repeatedly until nothing changes, so pieces that would join up into a new tag after one removal get caught too.
- A `<noscript>` block with a hidden payload may come out reshuffled, e.g. `<noscript></noscript><img src=x>"></noscript>`. It's safe, but it's the one place where some leftover text appears as visible content.

**Limitation:** filtering text like this is a best effort, not a guarantee. It covers the common and many obscure attack patterns, but it can't be proven to catch every trick a browser might fall for.
